Looking at this problem, I need to identify the graph-theoretic concepts and compute f(n) for n = 1 to 10.

## Setting Up the Translation

**"Interlinked"** network: among any 3 vertices, at least 2 share an edge → no independent set of size 3 → **α(G) ≤ 2**.

**"Radial Cluster" with n sectors**: vertex O connected to all A_i, B_i, with edges A_i–B_i for each i, all 2n+1 vertices distinct → this is the **windmill graph Wd(n)** (n triangles sharing a common vertex).

So **f(n) = R(Wd(n), K₃)**, the Ramsey number: the minimum N such that every graph on N vertices with α ≤ 2 contains Wd(n).

## Computing f(1)

Wd(1) = K₃. By Ramsey theory, **R(3,3) = 6**: every graph on 6 vertices has a triangle or independent set of size 3. The 5-cycle C₅ has α = 2 and no triangle, so f(1) > 5. Thus **f(1) = 6**.

## Computing f(n) for n ≥ 2: f(n) = 4n + 1

### Lower Bound: f(n) ≥ 4n + 1

Consider **G = K_{2n} ∪ K_{2n}** (disjoint union of two cliques) on 4n vertices:
- **α(G) = 2** (one vertex from each clique).
- **No Wd(n)**: every vertex has degree 2n−1 < 2n, but Wd(n) requires a center of degree ≥ 2n.

So f(n) > 4n, giving **f(n) ≥ 4n + 1**.

### Upper Bound: f(n) ≤ 4n + 1

Let G be a graph on 4n+1 vertices with α(G) ≤ 2. The complement Ḡ is triangle-free on 4n+1 vertices.

**Key Lemma**: If H is a graph on m vertices with α(H) ≤ 2, then H has a matching of size ⌊m/2⌋ when m is odd (since ≥ 3 unmatched vertices would form an independent set, contradicting α ≤ 2), and matching ≥ m/2 − 1 when m is even.

**Case 1: Some vertex v has degree ≥ 2n+1.**
Then |N(v)| ≥ 2n+1 and α(G[N(v)]) ≤ 2. By the lemma, G[N(v)] has a matching of size ≥ n. Together with center v, this gives Wd(n). ✓

**Case 2: All vertices have degree ≤ 2n.**
Then every vertex in Ḡ has degree ≥ (4n) − 2n = 2n. So Ḡ is triangle-free with minimum degree ≥ 2n.

For n ≥ 2: check 2n > 2(4n+1)/5 ⟺ 10n > 8n+2 ⟺ n > 1. ✓

By the **Andrásfai–Erdős–Sós theorem** (triangle-free graph on N vertices with min degree > 2N/5 is bipartite), Ḡ is **bipartite** with parts A, B where |A| + |B| = 4n+1 and |A|, |B| ≥ 2n. So {|A|, |B|} = {2n, 2n+1}.

Since Ḡ is bipartite, G contains cliques K_{|A|} and K_{|B|}. The larger clique has size **2n+1**. Any vertex v in it has ≥ 2n neighbors within the clique (forming K_{2n}), which contains a matching of size n. Thus **Wd(n) exists** with center v. ✓

Both cases yield Wd(n), so **f(n) ≤ 4n + 1** for n ≥ 2.

### Conclusion

**f(n) = 4n + 1 for n ≥ 2**, and **f(1) = 6**.

## Computing the Sum

| n | f(n) |
|---|------|
| 1 | 6 |
| 2 | 9 |
| 3 | 13 |
| 4 | 17 |
| 5 | 21 |
| 6 | 25 |
| 7 | 29 |
| 8 | 33 |
| 9 | 37 |
| 10 | 41 |

$$\sum_{n=1}^{10} f(n) = 6 + \sum_{n=2}^{10}(4n+1) = 6 + \left(4\cdot\frac{(2+10)\cdot 9}{2} + 9\right) = 6 + (216 + 9) = 6 + 225 = \boxed{231}$$

### PROOF COMPLETE
